# Rule Maintenance

Whenever you make a change that affects the project's architecture, conventions, or structure, **update the relevant `.cursor/rules/*.mdc` file(s)** in the same response before finishing.

## When to Update Rules

| Change made | Rule file to update |
|---|---|
| New page added, route changed, or nav link added | `common-tasks.mdc`, `project-overview.mdc` |
| New Firestore collection, service, or security rule | `firebase-backend.mdc`, `project-overview.mdc` |
| New translation key pattern or i18n behavior | `i18n-translations.mdc` |
| New coding convention, hook, or component pattern | `coding-conventions.mdc`, `react-components.mdc` |
| CI/CD, deploy, or environment variable change | `deployment-ci.mdc` |
| Tech stack, dependency, or project structure change | `project-overview.mdc` |

## How to Update

- Edit the specific section of the affected rule file — do **not** rewrite the whole file.
- Keep additions concise (match the existing style and tone).
- If a change spans multiple rules, update all affected files.
- If a new domain emerges that has no rule file yet, create one following the `.mdc` frontmatter format.

## What Not to Do

- Do not skip rule updates because the task "seems small" — small drift compounds.
- Do not create a new rule file if an existing one already covers the domain.
